why 2 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence in A minor where every resolution falls: soprano B–A, the alto's G♯ drops to E (the fifth), the seventh D falls to C and the bass to A.
why 3 1/2 SEVENTH The soprano's D belongs to the G chord, held over the bass's passing E. It is not a real seventh, and the tune rises to E as written.
why 6 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The same cadence as bar 2: soprano B–A, the alto's G♯ drops to E, tenor E–C and bass E–A, all falling into A minor.
why 9 1/2 DOUBLING The tune drops a fourth to A while the tenor keeps its A as a common tone. Bach keeps the held note rather than rearranging the chord.
why 9 1/2 LEAPS The bass climbs B–C–D–E–F by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 10 1/4 DOUBLING A varied cadential ⁶₄: the alto holds A over the bass's E as a suspension that falls to G♯, while B and D already belong to V. Heard as V with a held A, the bass needs no doubling.
why 10 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The same cadence formula as bars 2 and 6: all four voices fall into A minor.
why 11 1/2 LEAPS The bass resolves D♯ up to E and then leaps a fourth to A. The tritone D♯–A spans two chords, so it is not heard as one interval.
why 12 0 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to C while the tenor steps up B–C, and they meet in unison on the downbeat. The strong root move G–C matters more than strict spacing.
why 18 0 SPACING The tune reaches its high F while the alto stays low on C. The high point lasts a quarter, and the alto climbs C–D–E to close the gap.
why 18 1/8 SPACING The same gap, closing as the alto climbs C–D–E toward the soprano.
why 18 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (F–E) and alto (D–E) both move by step onto E; the alto keeps climbing to F and G. A doubled third for one eighth.
why 19 0 DOUBLING Over a held C in the bass the upper voices move to an F chord and back: a pedal ⁶₄, a neighbor chord over a held bass. Its doubling follows the upper lines, not the bass.
why 19 1/2 SPACING The tune leaps up to its high G for a moment while the alto stays in its middle range. The soprano falls back to F and E at once.
why 22 0 OVERLAP Alto and tenor both leap up, D–A and A–F, to place the F major chord higher. They move together, so the voices stay in order.
why 23 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (G–A) and tenor (B–A) both move by step onto A. Bach writes the nearest notes and accepts the doubled third.
why 23 3/4 DIRECT The tune falls C–A while the bass steps down E–D: a mild hidden fifth, softened by the step in the bass.
why 24 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The phrase ends on iv at the fermata. The chord arrives just before the bar line and is held into it, as a phrase ending does.
why 25 1/4 LEAPS The alto climbs B–C–D–E–F by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 25 3/8 SPACING The tenor drops to a low E and climbs back E–F–G while the alto rises. The wide gap lasts only a sixteenth or two.
